## Why This Is Asked

Assesses planning, risk management, data integrity, and cross-team governance for a major migration; tests ability to define milestones, ownership, and metrics beyond high-level talk.

## Key Concepts

- Migration strategy and data integrity checks
- Downtime avoidance and phased rollout (canaries, dual-write)
- Rollback criteria, SLAs, and incident readiness
- Cross-team governance and staffing plans
- Metrics dashboards and validation artifacts

## Code Example

```javascript
// Basic idempotent migration hook (illustrative)
async function migrateBatch(batch) {
  for (const record of batch) {
    try {
      await writeToNewPlatform(record);
      await markAsMigrated(record.id);
    } catch (error) {
      await logMigrationError(record.id, error);
      // Continue processing other records
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses your ability to align cost, risk, and velocity in a multi-team, multi-region environment with shared data platform infrastructure.

## Key Concepts
- FinOps and cost governance in cloud-native environments
- SLOs/SLIs and error budget policies
- Multi-tenancy isolation and privacy controls
- Budgeting, chargeback, and ownership models
- Incident costing and blameless postmortems
- Experiments to validate operational readiness

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tagging for cost ownership
function tagResource(resource, owner, project) {
  return {
    ...resource,
    tags: {
      owner: owner,
      project: project,
      costCenter: getCostCenter(owner),
      environment: process.env.ENV
    }
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design governance for large microservice ecosystems, including contract testing, versioning, auditability, and risk management under regulatory pressure.

## Key Concepts
- Contract-first API design and governance
- Versioning strategy and backward compatibility
- Contract testing integration (Pact) in CI
- Rollout strategies with canaries/flags and rollback criteria
- Immutable auditing and regulatory compliance

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pact contract test example (Node.js)
const { Pact } = require('@pact-foundation/pact');
const provider = new Pact({
  consumer: 'UserService',
  provider: 'OrderService',
  port: 1234,
  log: './pact.log',
  dir: './pacts'
});

provider.setup().then(() => {
  provider.addInteraction({
    state: 'user exists',
    uponReceiving: 'a request for user details',
    withRequest: {
      method: 'GET',
      path: '/users/123'
    },
    willRespondWith: {
      status: 200,
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: { id: 123, name: 'John Doe' }
    }
  });

  // Test consumer interaction against contract
  return axios.get('http://localhost:1234/users/123');
}).then(() => {
  provider.verify();
}).finally(() => {
  provider.finalize();
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates the ability to design governance that aligns regulatory requirements with product velocity in a distributed, multi-region setup.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-domain decision rights and escalation paths
- SLOs/SLIs, error budgets, runbooks, postmortems
- Phased releases: canary, shadow traffic, regional residency checks
- Rollback strategies and incident playbooks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-gating check for progress
function canProceed(region, changes) {
  if (!regsVerified(region)) return false;
  if (latencyImpact(changes) > SLO.latency) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate regulatory verification across regions?
- How would you handle conflicting gate criteria between regions?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a scalable reliability program that balances incident response with feature velocity and ROI.

## Key Concepts

- Platform vs product team governance
- SRE metrics: SLOs, error budgets, MTTR, and availability
- Incident playbooks, runbooks, blameless postmortems
- Auto-remediation, circuit breakers, canaries, multi-region failover
- ROI linkage and cost avoidance through reliability investments

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal auto-remediation skeleton
function autoRemediate(incident) {
  if (incident.severity === 'critical') {
    activateFailover();
  } else if (incident.type === 'timeout') {
    triggerCircuitBreaker();
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you prove ROI for reliability initiatives over time?
- Which metrics and milestones would you track to adjust SLOs?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to translate reliability into a product mindset across multiple teams, define measurable outcomes, and design practical experiments that balance speed and quality.

## Key Concepts

- Observability as a product
- SLOs and error budgets
- Instrumentation with OpenTelemetry
- Incident rituals and blameless postmortems
- Toil reduction experiments

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal OpenTelemetry trace init (illustrative)
import { trace } from '@opentelemetry/api';
const t = trace.getTracer('service');
const span = t.startSpan('request');
// business logic
